{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MyXjCjryXZcuYZiZdqd2k9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011CDC511Ai1QYqyMYY5zYnf","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","model":"opus","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **methodology and experiments**. \n\nReview the paper \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\" (7 pages, anonymous ACL submission).\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper introduces a counterfactual pairwise measurement procedure to detect style bias in LLM judges that Likert pointwise scoring misses. The pipeline: (1) LLM rewriting to generate content-preserving counterfactual pairs along a style axis (e.g., formality), (2) NLI-based semantic equivalence gate, (3) position-controlled pairwise comparison, (4) Likert scoring + binarized-Likert control, (5) original-preference control via double-rewrite.\n\nKey results across 3 judges (GPT-4o, Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash) × 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench):\n- Pairwise detects formality bias in 7/9 cells (p&lt;0.05); Likert in 1/9; binarized Likert in 0/9\n- Verbosity axis replicates the pattern (pairwise p=0.024, Likert p=0.18)\n- Register axis is exploratory (p=0.068, not significant)\n- Scaled replication at n=500: win rate stable at 0.693, p&lt;10^-5, Likert still non-significant (p=0.082)\n- Rewriter robustness: Llama-3.1-70B produces comparable results to Qwen3-32B\n- Human NLI validation: 91% agreement, κ=0.43, FP rate 1.1%\n- Original preference control: non-significant across all judges (mean WR 56%)\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what was planned)\nThe review_context.yaml listed these imagined experiments:\n1. exp-001: Multi-judge formality pairwise (3 judges on LLMBar) — COVERED in Table 1\n2. exp-002: Multi-benchmark formality pairwise (3×3 grid) — COVERED in Table 2\n3. exp-003: Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity analysis (9-cell comparison) — COVERED in Table 3\n4. exp-005: Original preference control (double-rewrite) — COVERED in Table 4, but only LLMBar shown (planned was all 9 cells)\n5. exp-006: Scaled replication n=500 — COVERED in Table 5\n6. exp-009: Non-Qwen rewriter control — COVERED in Table 6\n7. exp-013: Human NLI validation — COVERED in Table 7\n8. exp-007b: Verbosity full pipeline — COVERED in Table 8\n9. exp-008: Register axis — COVERED in Table 9\n\n## Your Review Tasks\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions on a 1-6 scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept):\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n- Is the counterfactual pairwise procedure well-designed?\n- Is the binarized-Likert control a valid diagnostic for isolating measurement structure vs. test choice?\n- Are the statistical tests appropriate (sign test for pairwise, t-test for Likert ATE)?\n- Is the NLI gate adequately validated?\n- Is the original-preference control sufficient?\n- Does the paper acknowledge limitations of its \"interventional\" framing (no DAG, no SUTVA)?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Coverage: How many of the planned experiments are reflected in the paper?\n- Are sample sizes adequate? (n=100 primary, n=500 replication)\n- Multiple comparisons: Holm-Bonferroni mentioned but p-values reported uncorrected — is this handled properly?\n- Is the 3×3 grid (Table 3) the strongest evidence? Any gaps?\n- Rewriter control: one alternative rewriter sufficient?\n- Human validation: n=100, κ=0.43 — adequate?\n- Verbosity: only GPT-4o×LLMBar tested, not the full 3×3 grid — limitation?\n- Register: exploratory, p=0.068 — appropriately framed?\n- Original preference control: Table 4 shows only LLMBar (3 judges), but text claims \"all 9 cells\" — discrepancy?\n\n### 3. Statistical Analysis\n- Is the sign test on consistent pairs appropriate, or does discarding inconsistent pairs introduce selection bias?\n- Is the binarized-Likert argument sound? Could there be other explanations for its insensitivity?\n- Power analysis claims (pairwise needs n≈80, Likert needs n&gt;400) — is this well-supported?\n- Effect sizes: Cohen's h=0.39 — properly computed and interpreted?\n- The GEE analysis for original preference (OR=1.65, p=0.072) — is this appropriately handled?\n\n### 4. Presentation of Results\n- Are the tables clear and informative?\n- Any missing figures that would strengthen the paper? (The paper has 9 tables but apparently no figures)\n- Is the narrative well-structured?\n\n## Output Format\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methodology &amp; Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentence summary of the paper's contribution from your perspective]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, each with a short label and explanation]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, each with a short label and explanation. Be specific about what's missing or problematic.]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list of specific questions you'd want answered]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Bulleted list of minor presentation/clarity issues]\n\n### Scores\n- Methodological Soundness: X/6\n- Experimental Rigor: X/6\n- Statistical Analysis: X/6\n- Clarity: X/6\n- Overall: X/6\n\n### Confidence\n[1-5 scale: 1=low, 5=expert]\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":34487,"cache_read_input_tokens":23660,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":34487},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f191c373-9bcf-4685-a04d-b4374a1ca49f","uuid":"ca0bbd3c-b60d-4ce5-b6cb-dec5d2c11b3c"}
