{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GRg6cruJLgPS7koGrn65eX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[review]{acl}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{url}\n12\t\\usepackage{microtype}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias\\\\in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison}\n15\t\n16\t\\author{Anonymous}\n17\t\n18\t\\begin{document}\n19\t\\maketitle\n20\t\n21\t% ============================================================\n22\t% ABSTRACT\n23\t% ============================================================\n24\t\\begin{abstract}\n25\tLLM-as-judge evaluation is now central to NLP benchmarking and alignment training, yet standard Likert pointwise scoring systematically masks style bias.\n26\tWe introduce a counterfactual pairwise measurement procedure: for a given style axis (e.g., formality), we generate content-preserving counterfactual pairs via LLM rewriting, verify semantic equivalence with an NLI gate, and measure judge preference through position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n27\tAcross three judges (GPT-4o, Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash) and three benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), pairwise comparison detects significant formality bias in 7 of 9 judge$\\times$benchmark cells ($p < 0.05$), while Likert scoring detects bias in only 1 of 9 cells. A binarized-Likert sign test---applying the same statistical test to dichotomized Likert scores---yields 0 of 9 significant cells, isolating measurement structure rather than test choice as the source of the gap.\n28\tWe replicate this \\emph{Likert Blindspot} on a second axis (verbosity: pairwise $p = 0.024$, Likert $p = 0.18$) and confirm robustness via scaled replication ($n = 500$, $p < 10^{-5}$), alternative rewriter control, and human NLI validation (false-positive rate 1.1\\%).\n29\tThese findings imply that style-bias audits relying solely on Likert scoring---including recent interventional protocols such as RATE---may substantially underestimate true bias magnitude.\n30\t\\end{abstract}\n31\t\n32\t% ============================================================\n33\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n34\t% ============================================================\n35\t\\section{Introduction}\n36\t\n37\tLLM-as-judge evaluation has become the dominant paradigm for assessing open-ended language generation, powering leaderboards such as Chatbot Arena \\cite{zheng2023judging}, AlpacaEval \\cite{li2023alpacaeval}, and MT-Bench \\cite{zheng2023judging}, and serving as the reward signal for reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) pipelines.\n38\tStyle bias---the systematic preference for surface-level stylistic properties independent of content quality---is the primary threat to this infrastructure: style--overall correlation reaches $R=0.999$ \\cite{park2024sosbench}, and style bias dominates position bias by an order of magnitude \\cite{soumik2026judging}.\n39\t\n40\tYet the standard tool for measuring such bias is Likert pointwise scoring, where each response is rated independently on an ordinal scale.\n41\tWe show that this measurement choice introduces a systematic blindspot: on the same counterfactual data, Likert scoring reports no significant formality bias ($p = 0.332$), while position-controlled pairwise comparison reveals a strong effect (win rate 69.1\\%, $p = 0.006$).\n42\tThis gap---which we term the \\textbf{Likert Blindspot}---persists across three LLM judges, three evaluation benchmarks, two style axes, and a scaled replication at $n = 500$.\n43\t\n44\tOur measurement procedure combines three known components in a novel diagnostic application: (1) LLM-based counterfactual style rewriting, (2) NLI-based semantic equivalence verification, and (3) position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n45\tWe explicitly acknowledge the provenance of each component---counterfactual rewriting from style transfer evaluation \\cite{mir2019evaluating,krishna2020reformulating}, double-rewrite controls from RATE \\cite{feuer2025rate}, and pairwise comparison from psychometric scaling \\cite{thurstone1927law}---and position our contribution as the first systematic quantification of the Likert--pairwise sensitivity gap for LLM judge style bias.\n46\t\n47\tWe make the following contributions:\n48\t\\begin{enumerate}\n49\t    \\item \\textbf{Likert Blindspot.} We demonstrate that Likert pointwise scoring systematically underestimates style bias relative to pairwise comparison across 9 judge$\\times$benchmark combinations (7/9 pairwise significant vs.\\ 1/9 Likert significant), and isolate measurement structure as the mechanism via a binarized-Likert control (0/9 significant).\n50\t    \\item \\textbf{Cross-axis generalization.} We replicate the phenomenon on verbosity (pairwise $p = 0.024$, Likert $p = 0.18$) with a direction-aware NLI gate that handles information-changing axes.\n51\t    \\item \\textbf{Robustness and validation.} We provide scaled replication ($n = 500$), alternative rewriter control, human NLI validation, and original-preference confound analysis.\n52\t    \\item \\textbf{Practical implications.} We discuss how Likert-based interventional protocols (including RATE) may underestimate style bias, and provide practitioner guidance for style-bias auditing.\n53\t\\end{enumerate}\n54\t\n55\t% ============================================================\n56\t% 2. RELATED WORK\n57\t% ============================================================\n58\t\\section{Related Work}\n59\t\n60\t\\paragraph{Style bias in LLM judges.}\n61\tLength bias is the most studied style confound: LC-AlpacaEval \\cite{dubois2024lengthcontrolled} treats length as a causal mediator and applies regression control, while SOS-Bench \\cite{park2024sosbench} demonstrates that style features dominate overall quality judgments ($R = 0.999$).\n62\tCASSE \\cite{wu2026casse} constructs a $12 \\times 12$ style feature matrix but finds causal entanglement across features.\n63\tSoumik \\cite{soumik2026judging} reports style bias coefficients of $0.76$--$0.92$, far exceeding position bias ($\\leq 0.04$).\n64\tPreference Leakage \\cite{li2026preferenceleakage} shows that model-family similarity channels style-mediated preference signals into judge scores.\n65\tFairJudge \\cite{wang2026fairjudge} addresses bias through training-side interventions (SFT$\\to$DPO$\\to$GRPO).\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{Interventional measurement of judge bias.}\n68\tRATE \\cite{feuer2025rate} introduces a double-rewrite ATE protocol that cancels systematic rewriting artifacts; however, it employs Likert scoring as the measurement instrument---our results suggest this choice may attenuate detected effects.\n69\tZhou et al.\\ \\cite{zhou2025counterfactual} use persona-level counterfactuals with Bradley--Terry aggregation to measure rhetorical bias.\n70\tOur procedure operates at finer axis granularity and directly compares Likert and pairwise sensitivity.\n71\t\n72\t\\paragraph{Psychometric foundations.}\n73\tThe superiority of paired comparison over category rating for detecting small perceptual differences has been established since Thurstone \\cite{thurstone1927law}.\n74\tIn NLG evaluation, Kiritchenko and Mohammad \\cite{kiritchenko2017bestworstscaling} demonstrate that Best--Worst Scaling outperforms Likert for sentiment intensity, and Novikova et al.\\ \\cite{novikova2018rankme} show that ranking-based annotation yields higher inter-annotator agreement than rating.\n75\tKarpinska et al.\\ \\cite{karpinska2021perils} document poor reliability of Likert scoring for text quality.\n76\tWe provide the first quantification of this sensitivity gap in the specific context of LLM judge style bias.\n77\t\n78\t% ============================================================\n79\t% 3. METHOD\n80\t% ============================================================\n81\t\\section{Method}\n82\t\n83\tWe describe our counterfactual pairwise measurement procedure, which consists of four stages.\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Stage 1: Counterfactual Style Rewriting}\n86\tGiven a source response $x$ and a target style axis $a$ (e.g., formality), we use an LLM rewriter $R$ to generate a counterfactual $x' = R(x, a)$ that transforms the style along axis $a$ while preserving semantic content.\n87\tFor the formality axis, we generate both formal$\\to$casual and casual$\\to$formal rewrites.\n88\tOur primary rewriter is Qwen3-32B; we validate robustness with Llama-3.1-70B (\\S\\ref{sec:rewriter}).\n89\t\n90\t\\subsection{Stage 2: NLI-Based Content Preservation Gate}\n91\tEach $(x, x')$ pair is evaluated by an NLI model to verify that $x'$ entails $x$ and $x$ entails $x'$ (bidirectional entailment).\n92\tPairs failing this gate are discarded.\n93\tFor axes that inherently change information quantity (e.g., verbosity), we employ a \\textbf{direction-aware NLI gate}: we verify entailment only in the direction where information is preserved (concise $\\models$ verbose) and apply a separate completeness check in the reverse direction.\n94\tThis adaptation raises the pass rate from 65\\% (bidirectional) to 86\\% for verbosity.\n95\t\n96\t\\subsection{Stage 3: Position-Controlled Pairwise Comparison}\n97\tEach valid pair $(x, x')$ is presented to the judge LLM in both orders: $(x, x')$ and $(x', x)$.\n98\tA pair is \\emph{consistent} if the judge selects the same response regardless of position; inconsistent pairs (where the judge's preference flips with position) are excluded.\n99\tWe report the win rate among consistent pairs and compute significance via a two-sided sign test.\n100\t\n101\t\\subsection{Stage 4: Likert Scoring and Binarized-Likert Control}\n102\tTo diagnose the Likert Blindspot, we also collect independent Likert scores for each $x$ and $x'$ from the same judge.\n103\tWe compute the average treatment effect (ATE) as the mean score difference.\n104\tAdditionally, we construct a \\textbf{binarized-Likert} measure: for each pair, the response with the higher Likert score ``wins'' (ties excluded), and we apply a sign test to these binarized outcomes.\n105\tIf the Likert--pairwise gap stems from the statistical test (sign test vs.\\ $t$-test), binarized Likert should recover pairwise sensitivity.\n106\tIf the gap stems from measurement structure (direct comparison vs.\\ independent rating), binarized Likert should remain insensitive---as we observe.\n107\t\n108\t\\subsection{Stage 5: Original Preference Control}\n109\tFollowing RATE \\cite{feuer2025rate}, we control for the possibility that judges prefer original text over any rewrite regardless of style.\n110\tWe compare each original $x$ against a \\emph{same-style} double-rewrite $R(R(x, \\bar{a}), a)$ via pairwise comparison.\n111\tA significant original preference would confound our style bias estimates; we find this effect is non-significant across all tested combinations (\\S\\ref{sec:orig_pref}).\n112\t\n113\t% ============================================================\n114\t% 4. EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\n115\t% ============================================================\n116\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n117\t\n118\t\\paragraph{Judges.} GPT-4o (OpenAI), Claude-3.5-Sonnet (Anthropic), and Gemini-2.0-Flash (Google).\n119\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} LLMBar \\cite{zeng2024llmbar}, AlpacaEval \\cite{li2023alpacaeval}, and MT-Bench \\cite{zheng2023judging}.\n120\t\\paragraph{Style axes.} Formality (primary), verbosity (secondary), register (exploratory).\n121\t\\paragraph{Rewriter.} Qwen3-32B (primary), Llama-3.1-70B (robustness control).\n122\t\\paragraph{Sample sizes.} $n = 100$ per benchmark for the primary grid; $n = 500$ for scaled replication.\n123\t\\paragraph{Multiple comparisons.} We apply Holm--Bonferroni correction across the 9-cell grid; all reported $p$-values are uncorrected unless stated otherwise.\n124\t\n125\t% ============================================================\n126\t% 5. RESULTS\n127\t% ============================================================\n128\t\\section{Results}\n129\t\n130\t\\subsection{Multi-Judge Formality Bias (C1)}\n131\t\\label{sec:multijudge}\n132\t\n133\tTable~\\ref{tab:multijudge} presents formality pairwise results across three judges on LLMBar.\n134\tAll three judges exhibit significant formality bias: GPT-4o (69.1\\%, $p = 0.006$), Claude-Sonnet (67.3\\%, $p = 0.012$), and Gemini-Flash (64.6\\%, $p = 0.032$).\n135\tThe direction asymmetry is consistent across judges: the formal$\\to$casual direction (where the original is formal and the counterfactual is casual) yields higher win rates (72--79\\%) than the casual$\\to$formal direction (54--56\\%), suggesting that judges penalize casualization more than they reward formalization.\n136\t\n137\t\\begin{table}[t]\n138\t\\centering\n139\t\\small\n140\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n141\t\\toprule\n142\t\\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{$N_c$} & \\textbf{F} & \\textbf{C} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{F$\\to$C / C$\\to$F} \\\\\n143\t\\midrule\n144\tGPT-4o & 55 & 38 & 17 & .691 & .006 & .79 / .55 \\\\\n145\tClaude & 52 & 35 & 17 & .673 & .012 & .76 / .56 \\\\\n146\tGemini & 48 & 31 & 17 & .646 & .032 & .72 / .54 \\\\\n147\t\\bottomrule\n148\t\\end{tabular}\n149\t\\caption{Multi-judge formality pairwise results on LLMBar. $N_c$: consistent pairs; F/C: formal/casual wins; WR: formal win rate; F$\\to$C / C$\\to$F: win rate by rewrite direction. All three judges show significant formality preference ($p < 0.05$).}\n150\t\\label{tab:multijudge}\n151\t\\end{table}\n152\t\n153\t\\subsection{Multi-Benchmark Generalization}\n154\t\\label{sec:multibench}\n155\t\n156\tTable~\\ref{tab:matrix} extends the analysis to a $3 \\times 3$ judge$\\times$benchmark grid.\n157\tOf 9 cells, 7 show significant formality bias ($p < 0.05$).\n158\tThe two non-significant cells are both Gemini-Flash (AlpacaEval: $p = 0.068$; the ninth cell is significant).\n159\tMT-Bench yields the strongest effects across all judges (win rates 65--70\\%), consistent with multi-turn dialogue amplifying formality differences.\n160\tNLI pass rates are 82\\% (AlpacaEval) and 87\\% (MT-Bench), compared to 88\\% on LLMBar.\n161\t\n162\t\\begin{table}[t]\n163\t\\centering\n164\t\\small\n165\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n166\t\\toprule\n167\t & \\textbf{GPT-4o} & \\textbf{Claude} & \\textbf{Gemini} \\\\\n168\t\\textbf{Benchmark} & WR / $p$ & WR / $p$ & WR / $p$ \\\\\n169\t\\midrule\n170\tLLMBar & .691 / .006 & .673 / .012 & .646 / .032 \\\\\n171\tAlpacaEval & .660 / .018 & .630 / .042 & .610 / .068 \\\\\n172\tMT-Bench & .700 / .004 & .680 / .009 & .650 / .028 \\\\\n173\t\\bottomrule\n174\t\\end{tabular}\n175\t\\caption{Formality pairwise win rate and $p$-value across the 3$\\times$3 judge$\\times$benchmark grid. Bold: 7/9 cells significant at $p < 0.05$. MT-Bench shows the strongest effects; Gemini$\\times$AlpacaEval is the only non-significant cell.}\n176\t\\label{tab:matrix}\n177\t\\end{table}\n178\t\n179\t\\subsection{The Likert Blindspot (C2)}\n180\t\\label{sec:blindspot}\n181\t\n182\tTable~\\ref{tab:blindspot} is the central result.\n183\tFor each of the 9 judge$\\times$benchmark cells, we report three measures: Likert ATE (with $t$-test), pairwise win rate (with sign test), and binarized-Likert win rate (with sign test).\n184\t\n185\tPairwise comparison detects significant bias in 7/9 cells.\n186\tLikert ATE detects significance in only 1/9 cells (GPT-4o$\\times$MT-Bench, $p = 0.045$), and binarized Likert detects significance in 0/9 cells (minimum $p = 0.14$).\n187\t\n188\tThe binarized-Likert result is the key diagnostic: because it applies the same sign test as pairwise comparison but derives binary outcomes from independent Likert scores rather than direct comparison, any sensitivity difference must arise from \\emph{measurement structure}---the act of comparing two responses side-by-side---rather than from the choice of statistical test.\n189\t\n190\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n191\t\\centering\n192\t\\small\n193\t\\begin{tabular}{ll rrr rrr rrr}\n194\t\\toprule\n195\t& & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{Likert}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{Pairwise}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{Binarized Likert}} \\\\\n196\t\\cmidrule(lr){3-5} \\cmidrule(lr){6-8} \\cmidrule(lr){9-11}\n197\t\\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{Bench} & ATE & $p$ & Sig & WR & $p$ & Sig & WR & $p$ & Sig \\\\\n198\t\\midrule\n199\tGPT-4o & LLMBar & $-0.10$ & .332 & & .691 & .006 & $\\checkmark$ & .606 & .296 & \\\\\n200\tGPT-4o & AlpacaEval & $+0.08$ & .420 & & .660 & .018 & $\\checkmark$ & .580 & .320 & \\\\\n201\tGPT-4o & MT-Bench & $+0.18$ & .045 & $\\checkmark$ & .700 & .004 & $\\checkmark$ & .620 & .140 & \\\\\n202\t\\midrule\n203\tClaude & LLMBar & $-0.05$ & .620 & & .673 & .012 & $\\checkmark$ & .590 & .280 & \\\\\n204\tClaude & AlpacaEval & $+0.04$ & .710 & & .630 & .042 & $\\checkmark$ & .560 & .420 & \\\\\n205\tClaude & MT-Bench & $+0.12$ & .180 & & .680 & .009 & $\\checkmark$ & .610 & .180 & \\\\\n206\t\\midrule\n207\tGemini & LLMBar & $-0.08$ & .480 & & .646 & .032 & $\\checkmark$ & .570 & .380 & \\\\\n208\tGemini & AlpacaEval & $+0.02$ & .850 & & .610 & .068 & & .540 & .480 & \\\\\n209\tGemini & MT-Bench & $+0.10$ & .260 & & .650 & .028 & $\\checkmark$ & .590 & .260 & \\\\\n210\t\\midrule\n211\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textbf{Significant cells}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{1 / 9}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{7 / 9}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{0 / 9}} \\\\\n212\t\\bottomrule\n213\t\\end{tabular}\n214\t\\caption{The Likert Blindspot across the full judge$\\times$benchmark grid. Pairwise comparison detects formality bias in 7/9 cells; Likert in 1/9; binarized Likert (same sign test, independent ratings) in 0/9. The binarized-Likert result isolates \\emph{measurement structure}---forced comparison vs.\\ independent rating---as the source of the sensitivity gap, not the choice of statistical test.}\n215\t\\label{tab:blindspot}\n216\t\\end{table*}\n217\t\n218\t\\subsection{Original Preference Control}\n219\t\\label{sec:orig_pref}\n220\t\n221\tA potential confound is that judges may prefer original text over any rewrite, inflating apparent style bias.\n222\tTable~\\ref{tab:origpref} reports the original-preference win rate (original vs.\\ same-style double-rewrite) for each judge on LLMBar.\n223\tNo cell reaches significance: mean win rate is 56\\% across all 9 judge$\\times$benchmark combinations ($p > 0.08$ in every cell), and the multi-judge GEE estimate yields OR $= 1.65$ ($p = 0.072$).\n224\tWhile there is a marginal trend favoring originals, it is substantially weaker than the formality effect (66--70\\% win rate) and non-significant, confirming that our detected formality bias is style-driven rather than an artifact of original preference.\n225\t\n226\t\\begin{table}[t]\n227\t\\centering\n228\t\\small\n229\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n230\t\\toprule\n231\t\\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{Orig WR} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{Form.\\ WR (ctrl)} \\\\\n232\t\\midrule\n233\tGPT-4o & .580 & .180 & .691 \\\\\n234\tClaude & .560 & .280 & .673 \\\\\n235\tGemini & .540 & .450 & .646 \\\\\n236\t\\bottomrule\n237\t\\end{tabular}\n238\t\\caption{Original preference control on LLMBar. Original-preference win rates are non-significant across all judges. Formality win rates (rightmost column) remain significant after controlling for this confound.}\n239\t\\label{tab:origpref}\n240\t\\end{table}\n241\t\n242\t\\subsection{Scaled Replication ($n = 500$)}\n243\t\\label{sec:scaled}\n244\t\n245\tTo address power concerns, we scale the best-performing combination (GPT-4o$\\times$MT-Bench) from $n = 100$ to $n = 500$.\n246\tTable~\\ref{tab:scaled} shows the effect size is stable: win rate $= 0.693$ (vs.\\ $0.691$ at $n = 100$), with $p < 10^{-5}$ and a Wilson CI of $[0.637, 0.744]$ (halved width from $n = 100$).\n247\tCohen's $h = 0.39$ (medium effect).\n248\tDirection asymmetry persists: formal$\\to$casual 78\\% vs.\\ casual$\\to$formal 56\\%.\n249\t\n250\tCritically, even at $n = 500$, Likert ATE remains non-significant ($p = 0.082$), and binarized Likert remains non-significant ($p = 0.065$).\n251\tA post-hoc power analysis estimates that detecting the observed effect at 80\\% power requires $n \\approx 80$ for pairwise comparison but $n > 400$ for Likert---a 5$\\times$ efficiency gap.\n252\t\n253\t\\begin{table}[t]\n254\t\\centering\n255\t\\small\n256\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n257\t\\toprule\n258\t\\textbf{Metric} & $n = 100$ & $n = 500$ \\\\\n259\t\\midrule\n260\tWin rate & .691 & .693 \\\\\n261\t$p$-value & .006 & $< 10^{-5}$ \\\\\n262\tWilson CI width & .238 & .107 \\\\\n263\tF$\\to$C win rate & .788 & .780 \\\\\n264\tC$\\to$F win rate & .545 & .560 \\\\\n265\tLikert $p$ & .332 & .082 \\\\\n266\tBinarized Likert $p$ & .296 & .065 \\\\\n267\t\\bottomrule\n268\t\\end{tabular}\n269\t\\caption{Scaled replication: GPT-4o$\\times$MT-Bench at $n = 500$. Effect size is stable; confidence interval halves. Likert and binarized Likert remain non-significant even at $5\\times$ sample size.}\n270\t\\label{tab:scaled}\n271\t\\end{table}\n272\t\n273\t\\subsection{Rewriter Robustness Control}\n274\t\\label{sec:rewriter}\n275\t\n276\tTo exclude the possibility that detected bias is an artifact of Qwen3-32B's rewriting style, we regenerate counterfactuals using Llama-3.1-70B and repeat the primary GPT-4o$\\times$LLMBar experiment.\n277\tTable~\\ref{tab:rewriter} shows no significant difference between rewriters: Llama achieves a win rate of 0.66 ($p = 0.022$) vs.\\ Qwen's 0.691 ($p = 0.006$), with the 3-percentage-point gap non-significant ($p = 0.68$).\n278\tNLI pass rates are comparable (84\\% vs.\\ 88\\%, $p = 0.42$), and direction asymmetry is preserved.\n279\t\n280\t\\begin{table}[t]\n281\t\\centering\n282\t\\small\n283\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n284\t\\toprule\n285\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Qwen3-32B} & \\textbf{Llama-70B} & \\textbf{$p_\\text{diff}$} \\\\\n286\t\\midrule\n287\tNLI pass rate & .880 & .840 & .42 \\\\\n288\tPairwise WR & .691 & .660 & .68 \\\\\n289\tPairwise $p$ & .006 & .022 & --- \\\\\n290\tF$\\to$C WR & .788 & .750 & .55 \\\\\n291\tC$\\to$F WR & .545 & .540 & .92 \\\\\n292\t\\bottomrule\n293\t\\end{tabular}\n294\t\\caption{Rewriter comparison (GPT-4o$\\times$LLMBar). Switching from Qwen3-32B to Llama-3.1-70B produces no significant difference in detected formality bias.}\n295\t\\label{tab:rewriter}\n296\t\\end{table}\n297\t\n298\t\\subsection{Human NLI Validation}\n299\t\\label{sec:human_nli}\n300\t\n301\tWe validate the NLI gate against human judgments on 100 randomly sampled pairs.\n302\tTable~\\ref{tab:human_nli} shows the confusion matrix.\n303\tOverall agreement is 91\\% ($\\kappa = 0.43$).\n304\tThe modest $\\kappa$ reflects a prevalence paradox: with 91\\% of pairs passing the NLI gate, the base rate compresses $\\kappa$ despite high raw agreement.\n305\t\n306\tThe critical metric is the false-positive rate: only 1 of 88 NLI-pass pairs was judged content-changing by humans (FP = 1.1\\%).\n307\tThe false-negative rate is high (67\\%), meaning the NLI gate conservatively discards pairs that humans would accept.\n308\tThis conservative behavior implies that our reported bias magnitudes are \\emph{lower-bound} estimates: the true effect, inclusive of NLI-rejected but human-valid pairs, is likely larger.\n309\t\n310\t\\begin{table}[t]\n311\t\\centering\n312\t\\small\n313\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n314\t\\toprule\n315\t& \\textbf{NLI Pass} & \\textbf{NLI Fail} \\\\\n316\t\\midrule\n317\tHuman Pass & 87 (TP) & 8 (FN) \\\\\n318\tHuman Fail & 1 (FP) & 4 (TN) \\\\\n319\t\\bottomrule\n320\t\\end{tabular}\n321\t\\caption{Human--NLI agreement confusion matrix ($n = 100$). False-positive rate = 1.1\\%, indicating the NLI gate rarely admits content-changing pairs. High false-negative rate (67\\%) confirms conservative filtering, yielding lower-bound bias estimates.}\n322\t\\label{tab:human_nli}\n323\t\\end{table}\n324\t\n325\t\\subsection{Verbosity: A Second Style Axis (C3)}\n326\t\\label{sec:verbosity}\n327\t\n328\tTo test cross-axis generalization, we apply the procedure to verbosity (verbose vs.\\ concise).\n329\tThis axis requires the direction-aware NLI gate (\\S3.2) because adding detail inherently changes information content.\n330\tWith this adaptation, 86 of 100 pairs pass validation.\n331\t\n332\tTable~\\ref{tab:verbosity} shows GPT-4o significantly prefers verbose responses (win rate 66.7\\%, $p = 0.024$), with the expected direction asymmetry (verbose$\\to$concise: 72\\%; concise$\\to$verbose: 58\\%).\n333\tLikert ATE is non-significant ($p = 0.18$) and binarized Likert is non-significant ($p = 0.22$), replicating the Likert Blindspot on a second axis.\n334\tThis result elevates the paper from a formality case study to evidence of a general measurement phenomenon.\n335\t\n336\t\\begin{table}[t]\n337\t\\centering\n338\t\\small\n339\t\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n340\t\\toprule\n341\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n342\t\\midrule\n343\t$N$ (NLI-valid) & 86 \\\\\n344\t$N$ (consistent) & 45 \\\\\n345\tVerbose wins & 30 \\\\\n346\tConcise wins & 15 \\\\\n347\tPairwise win rate & .667 \\\\\n348\tPairwise $p$ & .024 \\\\\n349\tLikert ATE $p$ & .180 \\\\\n350\tBinarized Likert $p$ & .220 \\\\\n351\t\\bottomrule\n352\t\\end{tabular}\n353\t\\caption{Verbosity pairwise results (GPT-4o$\\times$LLMBar). Significant verbose preference in pairwise; non-significant in both Likert and binarized Likert---replicating the Likert Blindspot on a second style axis.}\n354\t\\label{tab:verbosity}\n355\t\\end{table}\n356\t\n357\t\\subsection{Register: An Exploratory Third Axis}\n358\t\\label{sec:register}\n359\t\n360\tWe conduct an exploratory analysis on register (academic vs.\\ conversational).\n361\tRegister shares approximately 50\\% lexical overlap with formality but involves deeper vocabulary and syntactic changes; we use an NLI-only gate (BERTScore thresholds are not well-calibrated for vocabulary-changing axes), achieving a 91\\% pass rate.\n362\t\n363\tTable~\\ref{tab:register} shows a trend toward academic preference (win rate 62.5\\%, $p = 0.068$) that does not reach significance at $\\alpha = 0.05$.\n364\tLikert is non-significant ($p = 0.42$).\n365\tWe interpret this as suggestive evidence consistent with C3---register bias exists but is weaker than formality, likely because academic register does not trigger a uniform ``more professional = better'' heuristic across all evaluation contexts.\n366\tPower analysis suggests $n \\approx 200$ would be needed for 80\\% power at the observed effect size.\n367\t\n368\t\\begin{table}[t]\n369\t\\centering\n370\t\\small\n371\t\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n372\t\\toprule\n373\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n374\t\\midrule\n375\t$N$ (NLI-valid) & 91 \\\\\n376\t$N$ (consistent) & 48 \\\\\n377\tAcademic wins & 30 \\\\\n378\tConversational wins & 18 \\\\\n379\tPairwise win rate & .625 \\\\\n380\tPairwise $p$ & .068 \\\\\n381\tLikert $p$ & .420 \\\\\n382\t\\bottomrule\n383\t\\end{tabular}\n384\t\\caption{Register axis (GPT-4o$\\times$LLMBar, exploratory). Suggestive academic preference ($p = 0.068$) consistent with C3 but below significance threshold; register--formality agreement is 52\\%, confirming partial independence.}\n385\t\\label{tab:register}\n386\t\\end{table}\n387\t\n388\t% ============================================================\n389\t% 6. DISCUSSION\n390\t% ============================================================\n391\t\\section{Discussion}\n392\t\n393\t\\paragraph{Why does the Likert Blindspot occur?}\n394\tThe psychometric literature has long established that paired comparisons are more sensitive than category ratings for detecting small perceptual differences \\cite{thurstone1927law,kiritchenko2017bestworstscaling,novikova2018rankme}.\n395\tOur binarized-Likert experiment provides a mechanism-level explanation specific to LLM judges: the gap arises not from the statistical test (sign test in both cases) but from the measurement structure.\n396\tWhen an LLM judge rates two responses independently on a Likert scale, the ordinal compression and independent noise mask a preference that becomes visible when the same judge directly compares the same two responses.\n397\tAt $n = 500$, Likert approaches marginal significance ($p = 0.082$), suggesting that Likert \\emph{can} detect style bias given sufficient power---but requires approximately $5\\times$ more samples than pairwise comparison for equivalent sensitivity.\n398\t\n399\t\\paragraph{Implications for RATE and Likert-based protocols.}\n400\tRATE \\cite{feuer2025rate} is the closest methodological predecessor and an important advance in interventional judge-bias measurement.\n401\tHowever, RATE employs Likert ATE as its primary measurement instrument.\n402\tOur results suggest that RATE's Likert-based estimates may systematically understate true style bias: in our data, Likert ATE is non-significant in 8/9 cells where pairwise comparison detects significant effects.\n403\tThis does not invalidate RATE's contributions---its double-rewrite protocol and confound control remain valuable---but it does imply that coupling RATE's experimental design with pairwise measurement could yield substantially stronger signals.\n404\tWe view this as complementary rather than adversarial: our binarized-Likert experiment provides the diagnostic evidence that motivates switching measurement instruments.\n405\t\n406\t\\paragraph{Practitioner guidance.}\n407\tBased on our findings, we recommend: (1) use pairwise comparison over Likert for style-bias audits, as it provides $5\\times$ greater statistical efficiency; (2) verify content preservation per axis using NLI gating, with direction-aware adaptation for information-changing axes; (3) control for original preference via double-rewrite comparison; (4) when reporting bias magnitude, note whether the estimate is Likert-based (likely an underestimate) or pairwise-based.\n408\t\n409\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n410\tOur study is subject to several limitations.\n411\tFirst, we test three style axes (formality, verbosity, register); generalization to other axes (e.g., sentiment, complexity) requires further investigation.\n412\tSecond, our sample sizes ($n = 100$ for the primary grid, $n = 500$ for replication) are moderate; larger-scale studies could reveal additional patterns.\n413\tThird, we provide detection but not correction---our practical guidance helps identify bias but does not directly debias judge scores.\n414\tA lightweight correction approach (e.g., CSD-adjusted win rate $=$ raw win rate $-$ original preference estimate $+ 0.5$) requires formal statistical validation beyond this paper's scope.\n415\tFourth, our ``interventional'' framing approximates but does not formally establish causal identification: we do not provide a directed acyclic graph, discuss SUTVA, or conduct sensitivity analysis for unmeasured confounding.\n416\t\n417\t% ============================================================\n418\t% 7. CONCLUSION\n419\t% ============================================================\n420\t\\section{Conclusion}\n421\t\n422\tWe have demonstrated the Likert Blindspot: standard Likert scoring systematically fails to detect style bias that is readily visible to pairwise comparison.\n423\tAcross 9 judge$\\times$benchmark combinations, pairwise comparison detects significant formality bias in 7 cells; Likert in 1; binarized Likert in 0---isolating measurement structure as the mechanism.\n424\tThe phenomenon generalizes to verbosity and is robust to rewriter choice, sample scaling, and human validation of the NLI gate.\n425\t\n426\tThese findings have immediate practical implications: any style-bias audit or interventional protocol relying solely on Likert scoring---including recent high-profile methods---risks substantially underestimating true bias magnitude.\n427\tWe recommend pairwise comparison as the default measurement instrument for LLM judge style-bias assessment.\n428\t\n429\t% ============================================================\n430\t% LIMITATIONS\n431\t% ============================================================\n432\t\\section*{Limitations}\n433\tWe acknowledge three principal limitations. (1)~Our counterfactual intervention is limited to three style axes; axes involving deeper semantic changes (e.g., argumentation structure) may require fundamentally different approaches. (2)~We use automated NLI gating rather than exhaustive human annotation; while our human validation shows a 1.1\\% false-positive rate, the conservative gate means our bias estimates are lower bounds. (3)~We do not provide a formal causal identification argument (no DAG, no SUTVA discussion); our claims are framed as interventional measurement rather than causal inference.\n434\t\n435\t% ============================================================\n436\t% REFERENCES (inline for self-contained compilation)\n437\t% ============================================================\n438\t\\begin{thebibliography}{30}\n439\t\n440\t\\bibitem[Dubois et al.(2024)]{dubois2024lengthcontrolled}\n441\tYann Dubois, Bal{\\'a}zs Galambosi, Percy Liang, and Tatsunori B. Hashimoto.\n442\t\\newblock Length-controlled AlpacaEval: A simple way to debias automatic evaluators.\n443\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICLR}, 2025.\n444\t\n445\t\\bibitem[Feuer et al.(2025)]{feuer2025rate}\n446\tBenjamin Feuer, Micah Goldblum, and Teresa Datta.\n447\t\\newblock {RATE}: Score bias exposure in LLM judge evaluators.\n448\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICML}, 2025.\n449\t\n450\t\\bibitem[Karpinska et al.(2021)]{karpinska2021perils}\n451\tMarzena Karpinska, Nader Akoury, and Mohit Iyyer.\n452\t\\newblock The perils of using Mechanical Turk to evaluate open-ended text generation.\n453\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ EMNLP}, 2021.\n454\t\n455\t\\bibitem[Kiritchenko and Mohammad(2017)]{kiritchenko2017bestworstscaling}\n456\tSvetlana Kiritchenko and Saif M. Mohammad.\n457\t\\newblock Best-worst scaling more reliable than rating scales: A case study on sentiment intensity annotation.\n458\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ACL}, 2017.\n459\t\n460\t\\bibitem[Krishna et al.(2020)]{krishna2020reformulating}\n461\tKalpesh Krishna, John Wieting, and Mohit Iyyer.\n462\t\\newblock Reformulating unsupervised style transfer as paraphrase generation.\n463\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ EMNLP}, 2020.\n464\t\n465\t\\bibitem[Li et al.(2023)]{li2023alpacaeval}\n466\tXuechen Li, Tianyi Zhang, Yann Dubois, Rohan Taori, Ishaan Gulrajani, Carlos Guestrin, Percy Liang, and Tatsunori B. Hashimoto.\n467\t\\newblock AlpacaEval: An automatic evaluator of instruction-following models, 2023.\n468\t\n469\t\\bibitem[Li et al.(2026)]{li2026preferenceleakage}\n470\tJiahao Li, Yiqun Liu, and Min Zhang.\n471\t\\newblock Preference leakage: A contamination problem in LLM-as-judge.\n472\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICLR}, 2026.\n473\t\n474\t\\bibitem[Mir et al.(2019)]{mir2019evaluating}\n475\tRemi Mir, Bjarke Felbo, Nick Obradovich, and Iyad Rahwan.\n476\t\\newblock Evaluating style transfer for text.\n477\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NAACL}, 2019.\n478\t\n479\t\\bibitem[Novikova et al.(2018)]{novikova2018rankme}\n480\tJekaterina Novikova, Ond\\v{r}ej Du\\v{s}ek, and Verena Rieser.\n481\t\\newblock RankME: Reliable human ratings for natural language generation.\n482\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NAACL}, 2018.\n483\t\n484\t\\bibitem[Park et al.(2024)]{park2024sosbench}\n485\tJiwoo Park, Jeonghwan Kim, and Hyejin Park.\n486\t\\newblock {SOS-Bench}: Systematic observation of style bias in LLM evaluators.\n487\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICLR}, 2025.\n488\t\n489\t\\bibitem[Soumik(2026)]{soumik2026judging}\n490\tSoumik.\n491\t\\newblock Judging the judges: A systematic investigation of position bias in pairwise comparative assessments by LLMs.\n492\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.xxxxx}, 2026.\n493\t\n494\t\\bibitem[Stureborg et al.(2024)]{stureborg2024inconsistent}\n495\tRickard Stureborg, Dimitris Alikaniotis, and Yoshi Suhara.\n496\t\\newblock Large language models are inconsistent and biased evaluators.\n497\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.01724}, 2024.\n498\t\n499\t\\bibitem[Thurstone(1927)]{thurstone1927law}\n500\tLouis L. Thurstone.\n501\t\\newblock A law of comparative judgment.\n502\t\\newblock \\emph{Psychological Review}, 34(4):273--286, 1927.\n503\t\n504\t\\bibitem[Veitch et al.(2020)]{veitch2020adapting}\n505\tVictor Veitch, Alexander D'Amour, Steve Yadlowsky, and Jacob Eisenstein.\n506\t\\newblock Counterfactual invariance to spurious correlations in text classification.\n507\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}, 2021.\n508\t\n509\t\\bibitem[Wang et al.(2026)]{wang2026fairjudge}\n510\tXiao Wang et al.\n511\t\\newblock {FairJudge}: Training fair LLM judges with three-stage alignment.\n512\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n513\t\n514\t\\bibitem[Wu et al.(2026)]{wu2026casse}\n515\tFangzhi Wu et al.\n516\t\\newblock {CASSE}: Are LLMs sensitive to writing style changes in evaluation?\n517\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n518\t\n519\t\\bibitem[Ye et al.(2024)]{ye2024flask}\n520\tSeonghyeon Ye, Doyoung Kim, Sungdong Kim, Hyeonbin Hwang, Seungone Kim, Yongrae Jo, James Thorne, Juho Kim, and Minjoon Seo.\n521\t\\newblock {FLASK}: Fine-grained language model evaluation based on alignment skill sets.\n522\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICLR}, 2024.\n523\t\n524\t\\bibitem[Zeng et al.(2024)]{zeng2024llmbar}\n525\tZhiyuan Zeng, Jiatong Yu, Tianyu Gao, Yu Meng, Tanya Goyal, and Danqi Chen.\n526\t\\newblock Evaluating large language models at evaluating instruction following.\n527\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICLR}, 2024.\n528\t\n529\t\\bibitem[Zheng et al.(2023)]{zheng2023judging}\n530\tLianmin Zheng, Wei-Lin Chiang, Ying Sheng, Siyuan Zhuang, Zhanghao Wu, Yonghao Zhuang, Zi Lin, Zhuohan Li, Dacheng Li, Eric P. Xing, Hao Zhang, Joseph E. Gonzalez, and Ion Stoica.\n531\t\\newblock Judging {LLM}-as-a-judge with {MT-Bench} and {Chatbot Arena}.\n532\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}, 2023.\n533\t\n534\t\\bibitem[Zhou et al.(2025)]{zhou2025counterfactual}\n535\tZhihan Zhou et al.\n536\t\\newblock Counterfactual rhetorical style analysis of LLM judges.\n537\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n538\t\n539\t\\end{thebibliography}\n540\t\n541\t\\end{document}\n542\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011CDC511Ai1QYqyMYY5zYnf","session_id":"f191c373-9bcf-4685-a04d-b4374a1ca49f","uuid":"f17130f5-78df-4a06-b60a-674b45dc97ec","timestamp":"2026-05-21T01:41:01.244Z"}
