{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **novelty and related work positioning**.\n\nReview the paper \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\" (7 pages, anonymous ACL submission).\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper introduces a counterfactual pairwise measurement procedure to detect style bias in LLM judges that Likert pointwise scoring misses. The pipeline: (1) LLM rewriting to generate content-preserving counterfactual pairs along a style axis, (2) NLI-based semantic equivalence gate, (3) position-controlled pairwise comparison, (4) Likert scoring + binarized-Likert control, (5) original-preference control.\n\nCore claim (\"Likert Blindspot\"): Pairwise comparison detects formality bias in 7/9 judge×benchmark cells while Likert detects only 1/9 and binarized Likert detects 0/9. The binarized-Likert control isolates measurement structure (direct comparison vs. independent rating) as the source of the gap.\n\n## Key Related Work (from the paper + field knowledge)\nThe paper cites and positions against:\n- **LC-AlpacaEval (ICLR'25)**: Length as causal mediator, regression debiasing\n- **SOS-Bench (ICLR'25)**: Style-overall R=0.999, style dominates quality judgments\n- **RATE (ICML'25)**: Double-rewrite ATE protocol — the paper's primary methodological predecessor, which uses Likert ATE. Paper argues RATE's Likert measurement may underestimate bias.\n- **CASSE (2026)**: 12×12 style feature matrix, causal entanglement\n- **Soumik (2026)**: Style bias 0.76-0.92, far exceeding position bias\n- **Preference Leakage (ICLR'26)**: Model-family similarity channels style-mediated preferences\n- **FairJudge (2026)**: Training-side SFT→DPO→GRPO debiasing\n- **Zhou et al. (2025)**: Persona-level counterfactuals with Bradley-Terry\n- **Psychometric foundations**: Thurstone 1927, Kiritchenko &amp; Mohammad 2017, Novikova et al. 2018, Karpinska et al. 2021\n\n## Research Landscape Context\nFrom the distilled research skill for this project area:\n\n**Core consensus**: Style is the dominant LLM judge bias (R=0.999 style-overall, style bias 0.76-0.92 vs position ≤0.04).\n\n**Standard experimental patterns in the field**:\n- At least 3-5 heterogeneous LLM judges expected\n- Controlled intervention experiments valued over pure correlation\n- Bootstrap CIs + significance tests + multiple comparison correction expected\n- Semi-synthetic validation a rising trend\n\n**Known research gaps**:\n1. Multi-dimensional style causal decomposition (no one does joint causal decomposition across style dimensions)\n2. Style bias × Preference leakage interaction (studied independently)\n3. Debiasing downstream alignment impact (all papers stop at \"judge is more accurate\")\n\n**The paper's claimed contribution** fills gap #1 partially — it provides a measurement tool that detects per-axis style bias missed by Likert, but it does NOT do joint causal decomposition. It's more about measurement sensitivity than causal structure.\n\n## Your Review Tasks\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions on a 1-6 scale:\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is the core finding (Likert Blindspot)?\n  - The psychometric literature (Thurstone 1927) already established paired comparison &gt; category rating. The paper acknowledges this.\n  - The specific application to LLM judge style bias is new.\n  - The binarized-Likert diagnostic (isolating measurement structure vs. test choice) is a clever contribution.\n  - But: is this a \"known result in a new domain\" or a genuine contribution?\n- How novel is the methodology?\n  - Each component (counterfactual rewriting, NLI gate, pairwise comparison) is established.\n  - The combination is somewhat novel but not deeply original.\n  - The direction-aware NLI gate for information-changing axes is a minor methodological contribution.\n- Does the paper overstate its novelty? Lines 060-072 explicitly acknowledge component provenance. Good.\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage &amp; Positioning\n- Are key competitors properly cited and positioned?\n- Is the RATE comparison fair and accurate?\n- Any missing related work? Consider:\n  - FLASK (Ye et al., 2024) — cited in references but not discussed in text\n  - Stureborg et al. (2024) — cited in references but not discussed\n  - Best-Worst Scaling literature beyond Kiritchenko &amp; Mohammad\n  - Any work on Likert vs. pairwise in the NLG evaluation context specifically?\n- Is the \"first systematic quantification\" claim (line 136) justified?\n\n### 3. Significance &amp; Impact\n- How impactful is the finding for the LLM evaluation community?\n- Does the paper provide actionable guidance? (Yes: practitioner guidance in §6)\n- Does it provide detection but not correction? (Yes, acknowledged as limitation)\n- Would this change how practitioners audit for style bias?\n- Is the RATE critique constructive and well-calibrated?\n\n### 4. Scope &amp; Framing\n- Is \"Likert Blindspot\" an appropriate framing or overly dramatic?\n- Is the \"interventional\" language warranted given the lack of formal causal identification?\n- Are the four contributions (C1-C4) well-scoped and justified?\n- Does the paper's ambition match its evidence?\n\n## Output Format\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty &amp; Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentence summary of the paper's contribution from your perspective]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, each with a short label and explanation]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, each with a short label and explanation]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list of specific questions]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: X/6\n- Related Work: X/6  \n- Significance: X/6\n- Clarity: X/6\n- Overall: X/6\n\n### Confidence\n[1-5 scale]\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01G7YS3sTMGjm7udXT7ewmrx","session_id":"f191c373-9bcf-4685-a04d-b4374a1ca49f","uuid":"f2e94e4a-a27b-43ff-b12d-e081e0bfc2a8","timestamp":"2026-05-21T01:41:22.823Z"}
