{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A4QhX76kK2ZWQuBejN5eNb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage{array}\n13\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n14\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n15\t\\usepackage{microtype}\n16\t\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,citecolor=blue,linkcolor=blue,urlcolor=blue]{hyperref}\n17\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n18\t\\renewcommand{\\bibsection}{\\section*{References}}\n19\t\n20\t\\definecolor{siggreen}{rgb}{0.0,0.5,0.0}\n21\t\\definecolor{trendyellow}{rgb}{0.7,0.5,0.0}\n22\t\\newcommand{\\sig}[1]{\\textcolor{siggreen}{\\textbf{#1}}}\n23\t\\newcommand{\\trend}[1]{\\textcolor{trendyellow}{\\textit{#1}}}\n24\t\\newcommand{\\ns}[1]{\\textcolor{gray}{#1}}\n25\t\n26\t\\title{The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges\\\\via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison}\n27\t\n28\t\\author{Anonymous}\n29\t\n30\t\\date{}\n31\t\n32\t\\begin{document}\n33\t\\maketitle\n34\t\n35\t\\begin{abstract}\n36\tLLM-as-judge systems are increasingly central to NLP evaluation and alignment training, yet standard Likert pointwise scoring systematically fails to detect style bias.\n37\tWe introduce a counterfactual pairwise comparison protocol that generates style-varied rewrites of evaluation responses, verifies content preservation via NLI, and measures judge preference through position-controlled forced choice.\n38\tApplying this protocol to formality bias across three judges (GPT-4o, Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash) and three benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), we find that Likert scoring reports no bias in all nine conditions (0/9 significant), while pairwise comparison detects significant formality preference in two conditions (GPT-4o$\\times$LLMBar: 69.1\\%, $p{=}0.006$; Gemini-Flash$\\times$LLMBar: 63.8\\%, $p{=}0.041$) with supporting trends on AlpacaEval.\n39\tA scaled replication ($n{=}500$) confirms this \\textit{Likert Blindspot} is primarily a measurement efficiency gap: pairwise comparison requires ${\\sim}100$ samples for 80\\% power, while binarized Likert requires ${\\sim}680$ --- a ${\\sim}7\\times$ sample efficiency advantage.\n40\tWe provide power analysis tables, rewriter robustness checks, and practical guidance for practitioners auditing LLM judge style bias.\n41\t\\end{abstract}\n42\t\n43\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n44\t\\section{Introduction}\n45\t\\label{sec:intro}\n46\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n47\t\n48\tLLM-as-judge evaluation has become a core component of the NLP evaluation stack, powering leaderboards such as Chatbot Arena \\cite{zheng2023judging}, training data curation pipelines, and alignment reward signals.\n49\tA growing body of evidence shows that LLM judges exhibit systematic \\emph{style bias}: preference for responses with particular surface characteristics---verbosity, formality, markdown formatting---independent of content quality \\cite{sosbench2025,casse2026,soumik2026}.\n50\tThe correlation between response style and judge scores can be remarkably strong ($R{>}0.99$ for length in SOS-Bench), raising concerns that downstream systems optimize for style rather than substance."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01G7YS3sTMGjm7udXT7ewmrx","session_id":"f191c373-9bcf-4685-a04d-b4374a1ca49f","uuid":"b4d34738-646d-457d-89bd-c1ccfbce0e61","timestamp":"2026-05-21T01:42:09.755Z"}
